From: Catalyst Linux
Subject: [PATCH] panel-mipi-dbi-m: flushes timed by the tearing-effect line, where the Device Tree gives one

M5Stack's driver kept its TE code but compiled none of it. Switched on, and
used only when the panel's node has `te-gpios` (Catalyst Linux's portrait
overlay, which also has the panel take rows in the order it scans them):

- SET_TEAR_ON (vertical blank) as the panel is set up, and the interrupt on
  while the panel is on. Each rising edge only notes its time, which gives
  the scan's phase and, between two, its speed.
- Each flush starts where the scan will not cross its band of rows: just
  behind the scan when writing is slower than it (this panel's case), ahead
  of it when faster. How long a row takes to write is measured on the
  flushes themselves.
- The interrupt is disabled for real (IRQ_DISABLE_UNLAZY): the BCM2835 GPIO
  chip has no irq_disable, so a lazy disable kept the edges that came while
  "disabled" and replayed one the moment it was enabled again. That is why
  the first version, which enabled the interrupt to wait for one edge per
  flush, returned at once and still tore.
- A line that never moves (three flushes with no edge) is given up on until
  the panel is next switched on; flushes then go out as before.
- /sys/.../tearing_effect says how it is doing, for the shell.

Without `te-gpios` nothing changes.

